    $l13_master$> For five chapters the question was *where* things can go in space — the ellipse, the perturbation, the rendezvous, the interplanetary transfer, the Lagrange point. This one asks the question that closes the unit and turns it from celestial mechanics toward human consequence: what happens when too many things go to the same place, and no one owns the place? Low Earth orbit is a commons — shared, finite, un-owned — and it is filling with debris moving at speeds where a fleck of paint hits like a bullet and a bolt like a bomb. The physics of orbits meets the economics of the commons and the law of the sky, and the collision, literal and figurative, is the subject.

## The day two satellites hit at eleven kilometers per second

On February 10, 2009, at 16:56 UTC, 789 kilometers above the Taymyr Peninsula in northern Siberia, two satellites occupied the same point in space at the same instant. One was **Iridium 33**, an active American communications satellite, relaying calls. The other was **Cosmos 2251**, a defunct Russian military satellite, dead since 1995 — a two-thirds-of-a-tonne hunk of tumbling metal that no one was controlling. They were in nearly perpendicular orbits, and they met at a closing speed of about **11.7 km/s** — more than ten times the speed of a rifle bullet.

There was no explosion in the chemical sense; none was needed. At 11.7 km/s the kinetic energy alone is apocalyptic. The two satellites, a combined 1.5 tonnes, carried the energy of a large truck bomb, and when they met they were not dented but *vaporized* — converted in a fraction of a second into an expanding cloud of roughly **2,000 trackable fragments** and hundreds of thousands of smaller pieces, each still lethal. It was the first accidental hypervelocity collision between two intact satellites, and it happened not because anyone was careless in the moment but because two objects were in orbits that happened to intersect, in a sky where no one had the authority or the complete information to prevent it.

Here is the fact that contains the whole chapter: **the debris from that 2009 collision is still up there.** At 789 km, atmospheric drag is so feeble that these fragments will take *decades to centuries* to decay. Each is now an independent hazard on its own orbit, and each can *cause the next collision* — a 10-cm piece striking a working satellite would shatter it too, creating thousands more fragments. The 2009 event permanently raised the debris density in one of the most useful bands of low Earth orbit.

Two years earlier, in January 2007, something worse had happened *on purpose*. China tested an anti-satellite weapon by ramming a missile into one of its own defunct weather satellites, **Fengyun-1C**, at about 865 km. That single act created more than **3,000 trackable fragments** — the largest debris-generating event in history — spread into a shell around the entire planet at an altitude crowded with working satellites, and it will persist for *centuries*. One political decision, one missile, and a band of orbit was polluted for longer than any human institution has ever lasted.

These are not freak accidents. They are the early symptoms of a structural condition: low Earth orbit is a shared resource, finite and precious, filling with lethal debris that no one is obligated to clean up, moving at speeds where the smallest fragment is a weapon, governed by no one with the power to stop it. This is what the tragedy of the commons looks like when the commons is 800 kilometers up and the sheep move at eleven kilometers per second.

## Why a fleck of paint is a bullet

The danger of orbital debris is not obvious, and it drives everything. The key number comes straight from the two-body chapter: **orbital velocity in low Earth orbit is about 7.5 km/s.** Everything up there — every satellite, every spent bolt, every fleck of paint — moves at roughly this speed, and when two things on different orbits meet, they meet at a *relative* speed anywhere from a gentle closing rate up to nearly **twice** orbital speed (~15 km/s) for a head-on encounter, with a typical collision around **10 km/s**. This is *hypervelocity*, and at these speeds an impact is not like being hit by a fast object. It is like an explosion.

Kinetic energy scales as the *square* of speed, $KE = \tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$, and orbital speeds are so high that even tiny masses carry enormous energy. A **1-gram** paint chip at 10 km/s carries 50 kJ — more than a hand grenade's fragment. A **10-gram** bolt carries 500 kJ, over 300 times the energy of a rifle bullet: a single lost bolt would punch clean through a spacecraft wall and everything behind it. A **1-kilogram** fragment carries 50 MJ, about 12 kg of TNT — enough to obliterate any satellite it hits. This is why a fleck of paint travelling at orbital speed has chipped the windows of the Space Shuttle and the ISS: at 10 km/s a paint chip hits with the energy of a bullet. Spacecraft hulls are armored with **Whipple shields** — spaced outer layers that shatter and disperse an incoming fragment before it can penetrate — precisely against the small stuff that cannot be tracked.

```example
debris-energy
```

The relative velocity matters as much as the mass, and it is set by geometry. Two objects can both move at 7.5 km/s yet close at very different speeds depending on the angle at which their orbits cross: near zero if they travel together, $\sqrt{2}$ times orbital speed at a right angle, and up to *twice* orbital speed head-on. The closing speed is the vector difference $2v\sin(\theta/2)$ — the very same formula as the plane-change penalty, geometry rotating a velocity vector.

```example
debris-closing-speed
```

The debris environment, as of the mid-2020s, is roughly **35,000–40,000 objects larger than 10 cm** (tracked by ground radar — most of them dead satellites, spent stages, and collision fragments); about **1 million objects between 1 and 10 cm** (too small to track, too large to shield against — the most dangerous population, since each is lethal and none can be dodged); and some **130 million larger than 1 mm**, a constant sandblasting of everything in orbit. Crucially, every object is both a *potential victim* (it can be destroyed) and a *potential source* (when destroyed it becomes thousands of new fragments). This dual nature means debris can *reproduce* — and a hazard that reproduces is one that can run away.

## The Kessler syndrome: when collisions become self-sustaining

In 1978, the NASA scientist **Donald Kessler**, with Burton Cour-Palais, published a paper with a quietly terrifying thesis. As the number of objects in low Earth orbit grows, the collision rate grows too — not linearly but roughly with the *square* of the population, because collisions require pairs and the number of pairs among $n$ objects grows as $n^2$. Each collision produces fragments, which add to the population, which increases the collision rate further. Beyond a critical density, the process becomes **self-sustaining**: fragments from collisions cause new collisions faster than atmospheric drag removes debris, and the population grows on its own, without any new launches, in a runaway chain reaction. This is the **Kessler syndrome**.

The cascade logic has the same structure as a nuclear chain reaction or an epidemic. A collision creates a cloud of fragments; each fragment crosses other objects' paths at hypervelocity; some strike other satellites or debris; each strike shatters its target into thousands more fragments; those cause more strikes. The critical quantity is whether, on average, each collision causes *more than one* subsequent collision before its fragments decay — exactly the **basic reproduction number** $R_0$ of an epidemic. If $R_0 < 1$, the process fizzles: debris is created but drag removes it faster than it cascades. If $R_0 > 1$, it runs away — a self-sustaining chain reaction where the population grows exponentially, fed by its own fragments, until the orbit is so full that nothing can survive there.

```example
collision-pairs
```

And here is the truly frightening part: **once triggered, it cannot be stopped.** A nuclear chain reaction can be damped with control rods; an epidemic slowed with vaccines. But a Kessler cascade in a high orbit has no brake. The fragments cannot be recalled; they cannot be stopped from colliding; and the only natural cleanup mechanism, atmospheric drag, is negligible at the altitudes where the cascade is most dangerous. So a cascade there would run for *generations*, and there would be nothing to do but watch a band of near-Earth space fill with a shell of debris and become unusable — not for years, but for **centuries**.

### Why altitude is everything

The crucial variable is altitude, which ties the whole thing back to atmospheric drag. At **low** altitudes (below ~600 km) drag is strong enough to deorbit debris within years to a couple of decades, so even when collisions happen the fragments *decay away* before they can cascade, and $R_0$ stays low. The atmosphere is a free, natural debris-removal system down low — which is why the ISS at ~400 km sits in a relatively self-cleaning zone. But at **higher** altitudes (800–1200 km — unfortunately a hugely popular band for sun-synchronous imaging, communications, and now megaconstellations) drag is negligible and debris persists for centuries to millennia. There is no natural cleanup, so this is exactly where the Kessler syndrome is most dangerous — and, cruel irony, exactly where the most valuable satellites live. **The orbits humanity most wants to use are the ones the atmosphere cannot clean.** Drag, a killer in the drag-and-decay chapter (it brought down Skylab), is here a savior — the only janitor the commons has, and it only reaches down low.

Is the syndrome already under way? The honest answer is that we are at the edge, and parts of it may have already begun. Several studies — including by Kessler himself in later years — concluded that certain bands, particularly around 800–1000 km, may *already* be above the critical density, meaning the cascade is slowly self-sustaining there even now and would continue to grow even if all launches stopped tomorrow. The 2007 ASAT test and the 2009 collision each nudged those bands further. The cascade, if it has begun, is *slow* — it plays out over decades — but slowness is not safety; it is a catastrophe that cannot be seen happening in real time.

## The mitigation toolkit: what engineering can do

The problem is not that no one knows how to mitigate debris. The tools exist, and they are all applications of orbital mechanics.

**Deorbit at end of life.** A satellite in low orbit should, at end of mission, be brought down to burn up. If it is low enough, drag does the work; above ~600 km it must *actively* lower itself with a disposal burn — a small Δv (tens to hundreds of m/s to drop perigee into the atmosphere) — while it still has propellant. International guidelines require disposal within 25 years, recently tightened toward 5 in the US. The catch: it costs propellant, and it requires the satellite to still be functional at end of life. A satellite that dies suddenly cannot deorbit itself — like Cosmos 2251, which died in 1995 and became the 2009 collision partner.

**Graveyard orbits.** For geostationary satellites, deorbiting is prohibitively expensive — the Δv to drop from 35,786 km into the atmosphere is enormous. Instead, GEO satellites are boosted a few hundred km *up* into a graveyard orbit above the operational ring. This is cheaper, but it is not cleanup — it is moving the junk to a less-used shelf, a stopgap.

**Passivation.** A major source of debris is *explosions* of dead satellites and spent stages: leftover propellant, pressurized tanks, and charged batteries can rupture years later, fragmenting a dead object into thousands of pieces. Passivation — venting propellant, depressurizing tanks, discharging batteries at end of life — is cheap, now standard, and prevents a whole category of debris.

**Collision avoidance.** Active satellites can maneuver to dodge tracked debris. Ground radar predicts close approaches ("conjunctions") and operators fire thrusters to move aside; the ISS does this several times a year. But it works only against *tracked* debris (>10 cm), does nothing against the lethal 1–10 cm population, and the number of conjunction warnings grows as $n^2$ — operators are already drowning in alerts.

**Active debris removal.** The hardest and most necessary: sending a spacecraft to rendezvous with a dead satellite or spent stage and drag it down to burn up, using nets, harpoons, robotic arms, or a strap-on deorbit motor. Several missions have demonstrated pieces of this. But it is expensive, technically hard (grabbing a tumbling, uncooperative multi-tonne object at orbital speed is a serious rendezvous problem), and it raises the killer question: **who pays to remove debris that someone else created?**

Every one of these is an astrodynamics problem — deorbit burns and graveyard boosts are Δv budgets, collision avoidance is conjunction prediction and maneuvering, active removal is rendezvous with an uncooperative target. The engineer designing a satellite decides, in that design, whether it will passivate, whether it reserves deorbit propellant, whether it can maneuver. The tools all exist. So why is the sky filling with junk? Because knowing how to prevent it and being *obligated and incentivized* to prevent it are entirely different things — and the gap between them is not a problem physics can solve.

## The tragedy of the commons, at eleven kilometers per second

The intellectual heart of the chapter is not physics but economics. The reason low Earth orbit is filling with debris despite everyone knowing how to prevent it is a structural problem with a name and a long history: the **tragedy of the commons**.

In 1968 the ecologist Garrett Hardin described a scenario: a common pasture, open to all, on which anyone may graze cattle. Each herder reasons that adding one more cow brings *them* the full benefit while the cost — slight overgrazing of the shared pasture — is spread across *all* the herders, so they bear only a fraction of it. Therefore it is rational to add another cow, and another. Every herder reasons identically, and the pasture is overgrazed into ruin. **Each actor, behaving perfectly rationally, contributes to a collectively catastrophic outcome no one wanted.** That is the tragedy: not stupidity or malice, but individually rational choices summing to collective disaster when a finite resource is shared with no owner and no rules.

Low Earth orbit is the common pasture, and the structure maps exactly. The resource — usable orbital slots and altitude bands, and the low-debris condition of the commons — is shared and finite. The benefit of using it is private: an operator gets all the revenue from its satellite. The cost of degrading it is shared: debris that operator adds endangers *everyone's* satellites, so it bears only a sliver of the cost. Each actor is therefore individually incentivized to over-use and under-protect: launch more, spend less on deorbit and passivation, do not pay to clean up. Each choice is individually rational and collectively catastrophic. **This is why the sky is filling with junk despite everyone knowing how to prevent it.** The 2009 collision was not caused by a bad actor; no rule required anyone to remove Cosmos 2251, so no one did, and it sat there for fourteen years until it hit something. The tragedy is not a failure of individuals but a failure of structure.

In the language of economics, debris is a **negative externality** — a cost an actor imposes on others without paying it. When an operator launches a satellite and does not deorbit it, it imposes a cost (increased collision risk, a degraded commons) on every other user, but that cost never appears on its own balance sheet, so it "over-produces" debris relative to what would be socially optimal. It is the exact pathology of pollution: the polluter does not pay, so there is too much pollution. This reframes everything. **The debris crisis is not fundamentally a technology problem or a knowledge problem. It is an incentive problem — a mismatch between who benefits from using the commons and who pays for degrading it.** And incentive problems are solved not by engineering but by governance: rules, property rights, prices, treaties, enforcement — which, in orbit, mostly do not exist.

## The law of the sky: a treaty from 1967 and its discontents

What governance does space have? A remarkable, idealistic, and increasingly inadequate body of law written for a world that no longer exists.

The foundational document is the **Outer Space Treaty of 1967**, negotiated at the height of the Cold War when spaceflight belonged to two superpowers. Its core principles are genuinely visionary: space is free for exploration and use by all nations, with **no national appropriation** (no country may claim territory in space — it is "the province of all mankind"); no weapons of mass destruction in orbit; and **states bear responsibility** for their national space activities, including those of their private companies, and are **liable** for damage their space objects cause. That last point, elaborated in the 1972 Liability Convention, means that in principle if one country's satellite hits another's, the first country pays. In practice this has almost never been invoked — proving fault and causation after two satellites vaporize into thousands of fragments is a legal nightmare, and the 2009 collision produced no claim, partly because neither side wanted the precedent.

The treaty was adequate for its era, but it was written for **two spacefaring superpowers**, and the world now holds dozens of spacefaring nations and thousands of commercial operators launching tens of thousands of satellites. The mismatch is structural. **It establishes no property rights.** Because "no national appropriation" was meant to prevent colonial land-grabs, orbit is nobody's property — which sounds noble but is *precisely* the tragedy-of-the-commons condition: no slots anyone owns, no bands anyone is responsible for, no way to price the use of the commons. **The treaty's greatest virtue — that no one owns space — is also the root of the tragedy, because a thing no one owns is a thing no one protects. It has weak enforcement.** The debris-mitigation guidelines are exactly that: voluntary best practices, not enforceable law, with no orbital traffic authority empowered to compel anyone. **It did not anticipate the commercial explosion**, imagining a handful of government satellites rather than a single company launching tens of thousands. **And it does not touch the ASAT problem:** nothing prohibits a nation from destroying its *own* satellite with a missile — as China did in 2007, Russia in 2021, and others have — even though such tests create catastrophic, long-lived debris that endangers everyone.

So there is a governance gap of exactly the kind that makes tragedies of the commons intractable: a valuable shared resource, degrading through the summed rational choices of many actors, governed by a well-meaning but toothless framework with no property rights, no enforcement, no pricing of the externality, and no global authority. It is, in miniature and in orbit, the same structure as climate change and fisheries collapse: everyone would be better off if everyone cooperated, everyone is individually tempted to defect, and there is no world government to enforce cooperation.

## Is the tragedy inevitable? Ostrom's rebuttal

The tragedy of the commons is *not* a law of nature, and the most important development in this field is the reason why. When Hardin published in 1968, his bleak conclusion — that commons are doomed without either privatization or top-down government control — became conventional wisdom. But it was challenged, and substantially overturned, by the political economist **Elinor Ostrom**, whose work won the 2009 Nobel Prize in Economics.

Ostrom studied real commons all over the world — fisheries, forests, irrigation systems, grazing lands — and found Hardin *empirically wrong* in many cases: communities frequently *do* manage shared resources successfully, without either privatizing them or imposing external control, through **self-organized governance** — locally-agreed rules, monitoring, graduated sanctions for violators, and mechanisms for collective decision-making. She identified design principles for success: clearly defined boundaries (who may use the resource), rules matched to local conditions, collective-choice participation, monitoring, graduated sanctions, conflict resolution, and recognition of the community's right to organize. **The tragedy of the commons is not inevitable; it is the outcome of a particular institutional failure, and other institutions can avert it.**

This reframes the challenge. The question is not "can a commons be saved?" — Ostrom showed it can — but "can the *institutions* be built to govern the orbital commons before it cascades?" The orbital commons is a hard case for her principles: the "community" is sovereign nations and competing corporations rather than a village of neighbors; boundaries are ill-defined; monitoring is technically hard; and there is no shared enforcement. A village fishery has a small, stable, interdependent community with shared norms and local enforcement; the orbital commons has none of these. But hard is not hopeless, and Ostrom's work says where to look: not only at technology, but at institution-building.

Several paths are being pursued or proposed, none sufficient alone. **Binding rules with enforcement** turn the voluntary deorbit guidelines into licensing conditions with financial teeth — national regulators such as the FCC are beginning to do this, using the leverage of launch and spectrum licensing that nations *do* control. **Pricing the externality** — an orbital-use fee proportional to a satellite's mass, altitude, and maneuverability — puts the cost of degrading the commons on the operator's balance sheet (the economist's classic Pigouvian fix). **Space traffic management**, like air traffic control, would track all objects, predict conjunctions, and coordinate maneuvers with some authority. **Active removal, funded collectively** treats cleanup as a public good, since no operator will pay to remove debris it did not create. And **ASAT test bans** would prohibit the debris-generating tests that are the worst single events, building on the voluntary moratoria several nations have already adopted. None is easy; all require collective action among competing sovereigns. But they are paths, and Ostrom's work proves the paths can lead somewhere.

## The commons, and the end of Unit II

Unit II began with a single ellipse around a single planet and ends with the question of whether humanity can share the space around that planet at all. That arc is the deep structure of the unit. Its earlier chapters taught, with growing power, *how to use space* — the two-body orbit, the tuned perturbation, the counterintuitive rendezvous, the interplanetary reach that steals energy from planets, and the unsolvable three-body frontier with its Lagrange points. This chapter confronts the consequence: humanity has become so good at putting things in orbit that it is on the verge of ruining orbit for everyone, and the tools that made us masters of trajectory are useless against a problem that is not about trajectory at all.

The physics of the crisis is pure Unit II. Debris is lethal because orbital velocity is 7.5 km/s and kinetic energy scales as velocity squared, so a fleck of paint hits like a bullet and a bolt like a bomb. Debris persists because drag is negligible above ~600 km — the atmosphere, our only free janitor, cannot reach the orbits we most want. Collisions cascade because each fragment is both victim and source, and above the critical density the Kessler syndrome runs away like a chain reaction with no brake, for centuries. And the mitigation tools — deorbit burns, graveyard orbits, collision avoidance, active removal — are all astrodynamics. The engineer who understands orbits understands both the disease and the cure.

But the disease has no engineering cure, because it is not fundamentally an engineering problem. It is a **tragedy of the commons** — an incentive problem, not a technology problem. Orbit is a finite, shared, un-owned resource; each operator captures the private benefit of using it while imposing the cost of degrading it on everyone; so each rationally under-invests in protection, and the sum slides toward cascade. Debris is orbital pollution, and the polluter does not pay. The governance we have is a visionary 1967 treaty written for two superpowers, establishing no property rights, no enforcement, no externality pricing, straining under a commercial reality it never imagined — its noblest principle, that no one owns space, being the very root of the tragedy. And yet it is not hopeless: Ostrom proved that a commons can be governed, and the paths exist. Whether we walk them before a cascade forecloses them will be decided not by physicists but by engineers, economists, lawyers, and diplomats together — which is the deepest reason the subject belongs in an engineering education.

The through-line of the whole course reaches its final and largest form here. *Never carry anything through a Δv it does not need to experience* — the discipline of not wasting what is scarce — becomes **never leave in orbit anything that does not need to be there.** The dead satellite, the spent stage, the un-deorbited hulk are the ultimate dead mass, coasting endlessly through the shared sky, and the cost is borne not by one rocket's payload but by all of humanity's access to space and by the generations to come. The principle that began as efficiency (drop the dead tank) ends as stewardship (drop your dead satellite; do not foul the commons). That an astrodynamics course closes not with an equation but with a question of collective responsibility is exactly right: the orbit you fill is not yours. It is held in trust, the trust is fraying, and repairing it is now part of the job. Unit II taught how to navigate the heavens; its final lesson is that navigating them well is not only a technical achievement but a moral and institutional one — and that the sky, like every commons, is kept only by those who choose to keep it.

## Further reading

- **Donald J. Kessler & Burton G. Cour-Palais, "Collision Frequency of Artificial Satellites: The Creation of a Debris Belt" (1978).** The founding paper on collisional cascading — remarkably readable, and the origin of the syndrome that bears Kessler's name.
- **Garrett Hardin, "The Tragedy of the Commons" (1968).** The essay that named the structural problem at the heart of the debris crisis; short, provocative, and still argued over.
- **Elinor Ostrom, *Governing the Commons*.** The Nobel-winning rebuttal to Hardin — how real communities govern shared resources without privatization or top-down control, and the design principles that make it work.
